1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
It's eleven at night, and you're the last

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,220
person in the laundromat.

3
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:05,980
On top of the lost-and-found box there's a

4
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:08,060
black notebook with two words on the cover:

5
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:09,080
Death Note.

6
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:11,620
Inside, the first page is a list of

7
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:13,740
rules, written in careful English.

8
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,680
The first one says: the human whose name

9
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:18,240
is written in this note shall die.

10
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:19,200
You laugh.

11
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:20,800
Then you read the rest.

12
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:22,380
Then you stop laughing.

13
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:24,900
In the story, this notebook belongs to the

14
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,800
shinigami, the gods of death, and it changes

15
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:29,920
everything for a high-school student called Light.

16
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:31,360
You're not Light.

17
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:33,760
You've just got the notebook, a wet bag

18
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:35,770
of laundry, and a choice you're going to

19
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:37,300
have to make every single day.

20
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:40,820
You read the rules at your kitchen table

21
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:42,340
until two in the morning.

22
00:00:43,020 --> 00:00:44,680
If you write a name and nothing else,

23
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:46,740
the person dies of a heart attack forty

24
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:47,440
seconds later.

25
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:49,820
If you write a cause, you have six

26
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:52,120
minutes and forty seconds to add the details.

27
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:54,460
And you have to know their face.

28
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:56,280
A name alone doesn't work.

29
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:58,660
You have to picture the person while you

30
00:00:58,660 --> 00:00:58,880
write.

31
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:00,800
You close the notebook.

32
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:02,720
You don't write anything.

33
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:03,900
Rule one:

34
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:05,600
a name is not enough.

35
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,660
The notebook needs a face.

36
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:11,460
Everybody who hears this story asks the same

37
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:12,040
question:

38
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:13,460
how do you know it's real?

39
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:15,900
In the manga, Light tests it straight away.

40
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:16,880
You don't.

41
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:18,820
You sit in a cafe with the notebook

42
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:20,620
in your bag, and every time someone is

43
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:22,460
rude to the waiter, you feel it there,

44
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:23,220
like a weight.

45
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:26,460
You realise something that isn't in any rule:

46
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:28,940
the notebook doesn't need you to use it

47
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:29,720
to change you.

48
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:31,920
Just having it changes how you look at

49
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:32,180
people.

50
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:35,280
Rule two: the most dangerous thing about the

51
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:37,280
notebook is the idea of it.

52
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:40,760
On day five, the news has a strange

53
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:41,240
story.

54
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:44,820
Three men, all on trial, all died of

55
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:46,400
heart attacks on the same afternoon.

56
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:48,840
The reporter calls it a coincidence.

57
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:50,760
You look at your notebook.

58
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:51,960
It's closed.

59
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:53,660
You didn't write anything.

60
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:56,900
Which means there's another one.

61
00:01:57,560 --> 00:02:00,580
Rule three: you are not the only one.

62
00:02:01,580 --> 00:02:04,600
By day nine, there are twenty-one heart attacks,

63
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:06,680
and every single one is someone the news

64
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:07,480
has talked about.

65
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:09,720
And this is where the real world comes

66
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:10,060
in.

67
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:13,380
In Britain, a doctor called Harold Shipman killed

68
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:15,320
his own patients for years.

69
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:18,120
An official inquiry first concluded he had killed

70
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:21,960
215 people, and later estimated about 250.

71
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:25,280
What raised the first alarm wasn't a confession.

72
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:28,480
It was other doctors, who noticed that his

73
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:30,580
patients were dying unusually often.

74
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:34,680
Afterwards, statisticians argued that simply watching the death

75
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:36,760
rates would have caught him years earlier.

76
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:41,200
Someone, somewhere, is about to start watching the

77
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:41,800
death rates.

78
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:44,820
Rule four: a single death is a mystery.

79
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:47,080
A pattern is evidence.

80
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:50,600
On day fourteen the whole city is talking

81
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:51,200
about it.

82
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:52,420
Some people are scared.

83
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:54,860
Some people are cheering, and that scares you

84
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:55,100
more.

85
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:57,080
The police hold a press conference.

86
00:02:57,620 --> 00:02:59,400
A detective explains that they are treating it

87
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:00,160
as a series.

88
00:03:00,860 --> 00:03:03,220
They're looking at who died, when, and who

89
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:03,940
knew their faces.

90
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:06,900
In real life, how good police are at

91
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,740
solving murders depends a lot on the country.

92
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:11,340
One study in Japan put the solved rate

93
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:13,600
at about 95 percent.

94
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:15,780
In the United States in 2023, it was

95
00:03:15,780 --> 00:03:17,840
about 52 percent.

96
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:19,520
The numbers aren't counted the same way, but

97
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:21,840
either way, a city full of heart attacks

98
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:24,280
is not the kind of thing investigators ignore.

99
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:26,040
Rule five:

100
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:28,740
the more you use it, the louder it

101
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:29,080
gets.

102
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:32,940
On day seventeen, your best friend comes over,

103
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:34,520
and when you walk back in with two

104
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:36,080
coffees, they're holding the notebook.

105
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:37,500
They're laughing.

106
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:38,860
"Death Note?

107
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:39,940
Is this a joke?"

108
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:42,420
They flick through the rules out loud, doing

109
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:43,480
a spooky voice.

110
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:46,280
You take it back, a bit too fast.

111
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:47,580
They stop laughing.

112
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:48,600
"Wait.

113
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:51,680
You're not, like, taking this seriously?"

114
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:53,300
You say no.

115
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:55,300
You say it's a prop from a party.

116
00:03:55,980 --> 00:03:57,420
And for the first time in your life,

117
00:03:58,000 --> 00:03:59,820
you lie to your best friend without even

118
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:00,680
thinking about it.

119
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:02,740
That night you realise the notebook has already

120
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:03,440
cost you something,

121
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:05,440
and you haven't even opened it.

122
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:08,680
On day twenty, something is standing in the

123
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:09,580
corner of your room.

124
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:10,480
Tall.

125
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:11,360
Thin.

126
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:13,000
Its eyes glow red.

127
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:15,640
It's the shinigami who owns your notebook,

128
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:17,260
and it has an offer.

129
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:19,920
It can give you the shinigami eyes.

130
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:23,160
With them, you'd see anyone's name just by

131
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:24,060
looking at their face.

132
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:26,200
You'd know who the other owner is in

133
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:26,580
a second.

134
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:28,960
The price is half of the life you

135
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:29,380
have left.

136
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:32,440
In the story, someone takes that deal, and

137
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:34,580
then takes it again, and each time it

138
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:35,480
halves what's left.

139
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:37,060
You think about it all night.

140
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:38,300
You say no.

141
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:41,880
Rule six: every shortcut in this notebook costs

142
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:42,900
more than it saves.

143
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:46,880
On day twenty-three you find a page you

144
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:47,520
hadn't noticed.

145
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:49,240
A rule in a different handwriting:

146
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:51,980
if the owner doesn't write a name every

147
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:54,220
thirteen days, the owner dies.

148
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:56,320
For a second, your heart stops.

149
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:57,600
Then you remember.

150
00:04:58,180 --> 00:04:59,580
In the story, that rule is fake.

151
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:01,940
Light wrote it himself, to fool the police.

152
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:05,140
Someone has done the same thing here, to

153
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:07,880
scare whoever finds the notebook into using it.

154
00:05:08,380 --> 00:05:09,420
You almost fell for it.

155
00:05:09,720 --> 00:05:11,400
That's the thing about a notebook like this.

156
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:12,760
It isn't only deadly.

157
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:14,300
It's persuasive.

158
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:18,220
On day twenty-six, the detective calls you back.

159
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:19,740
They want to talk in person.

160
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:22,660
The interview room is small and too warm.

161
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:25,460
The detective has a folder full of printouts:

162
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:28,800
every death, every date, every face that was

163
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:29,280
on the news.

164
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:31,940
"You were very helpful on the phone," they

165
00:05:31,940 --> 00:05:32,160
say.

166
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:33,700
"How did you know so much?"

167
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:35,260
You tell them you read the news.

168
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:37,580
You tell them you noticed the pattern, like

169
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:39,300
those doctors who noticed the death rates.

170
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:41,540
They look at you for a long time.

171
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:42,640
Then they smile,

172
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:45,260
slide a card across the table, and say:

173
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:48,440
"If you notice anything else, call me first."

174
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:50,580
You walk out into the cold air with

175
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:51,580
your heart pounding.

176
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:54,400
Because you realise the detective is doing exactly

177
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:55,080
what you did.

178
00:05:55,080 --> 00:05:56,580
They're watching the pattern.

179
00:05:56,580 --> 00:06:00,060
And one day, the pattern might include you.

180
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:03,140
On day thirty, you work it out.

181
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:06,260
The other owner is someone you know.

182
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:07,560
You have their name.

183
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:10,380
You have their face, in a photo on

184
00:06:10,380 --> 00:06:10,740
your phone.

185
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:14,140
You could end this in forty seconds.

186
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:16,820
You sit on the roof with the notebook

187
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:18,680
open on your knees and a pen in

188
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:20,340
your hand for a long time.

189
00:06:20,340 --> 00:06:22,940
The city lights come on one by one.

190
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:25,240
And you realise that the moment you write

191
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:28,040
that name, you become exactly the thing you

192
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:28,760
were trying to stop.

193
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:30,120
You close the notebook.

194
00:06:30,820 --> 00:06:32,540
You go downstairs, and you call the number

195
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:33,440
from the press conference.

196
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:37,240
You tell the detective everything you know, except

197
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:39,400
one thing: that you have a notebook too.

198
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:41,980
The next night you go back to the

199
00:06:41,980 --> 00:06:44,060
laundromat to put the notebook back where you

200
00:06:44,060 --> 00:06:44,680
found it.

201
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:46,640
You hold it over the box for a

202
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:46,800
minute.

203
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:48,780
And you can't let go.

204
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:51,980
Because whoever finds it next might not close

205
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:52,280
it.

206
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:53,360
So you keep it.

207
00:06:53,940 --> 00:06:54,480
In a drawer.

208
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:55,620
Unopened.

209
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:58,040
That's the real cost of the Death Note.

210
00:06:58,740 --> 00:06:59,720
Not half your life.

211
00:06:59,940 --> 00:07:01,660
Not a heart attack in forty seconds.

212
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:04,080
It's that once you know it exists,

213
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:06,720
you can never stop being the person who

214
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:07,980
decides not to use it,

215
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:10,060
every single day.

216
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:13,000
So, if you found this notebook tomorrow: would

217
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:13,460
you write a name,

218
00:07:14,060 --> 00:07:15,260
or would you keep it closed forever?

219
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:17,780
And which rule scares you most?

220
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:19,320
Tell us in the comments.
